---
// Two short blocks: for people coming from Cabbage 2, and for people who
// sell what they make.
import { site } from '../../data/site';
---

<section class="cb-section">
  <div class="container two-up">
    <div>
      <h2 class="cb-h3-lg">Coming from Cabbage 2?</h2>
      <p class="cb-muted">
        Cabbage 3 is a rewrite, and some familiar things have moved. The standalone IDE has given way to a VS Code
        extension, widgets are written as JSON with camelCase names, and instruments run on Csound 7. One command
        converts your old widget code.
      </p>
      <div class="two-up__links">
        <a class="textlink" href="/docs/whats-new/">What is new in Cabbage 3</a>
        <a class="textlink" href={site.v2Url}>Cabbage 2 downloads and docs</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2 class="cb-h3-lg">Selling what you make</h2>
      <p class="cb-muted">
        Instruments you build with Cabbage are yours to distribute and sell. In a standard export, end users can read
        your Csound code. If you need to ship it encrypted, Cabbage Pro adds protected exports. Message rorywalsh on the
        forum for details.
      </p>
      <div class="two-up__links">
        <a class="textlink" href="/docs/faqs/">Read the FAQs</a>
        <a class="textlink" href={site.forumUrl}>Ask about Cabbage Pro</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .two-up {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(440px, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: 48px 80px;
    padding-block: clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px);
  }
  .two-up > div {
    min-width: 0;
  }
  p {
    margin-top: 12px;
  }
  .two-up__links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0 28px;
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
</style>
